// exit() in a #run ends the compiler there (spec 7.8), after printing the
// errors found so far. f's x: X, found before X is worked out, is "'X' is
// not a type", which only repeats X's own mistake: it's withdrawn before
// they're printed, as at the end of a check that isn't cut short (spec
// 3.22), and X's mistake is said once.
exit :: (status: s32) #foreign libc;
f :: (x: X) { }
X :: 1 + "a";
stop :: () -> int { exit(1); return 0; }
N :: #run stop();
main :: () { }
